<!--
  ContentComponent.wxs
  ====================
  Detection content for the PhantomHome MSI.

  Contents:
    CmpDetectionContent  - signatures.sdb and THIRD-PARTY-RULES.md staged into
                           [INSTALLFOLDER]\content\.
    CmpProgramDataData   - creates ProgramData\ShadowStrike\Data\ with the ACL
                           the service expects.

  WHY THE BASELINE LIVES IN PROGRAM FILES AND NOT IN PROGRAMDATA
  ==============================================================
  The service reads its database from ProgramData\ShadowStrike\Data\signatures.sdb,
  so the obvious move is to install it straight there. That is wrong for two
  reasons.

  First, ownership. That file is runtime state: the signature updater and the feed
  subsystem are meant to replace it in place. If Windows Installer also owns it,
  then every repair, upgrade and uninstall fights whatever the product has done
  since install, and MSI's rules for unversioned files - compare timestamps, skip
  if modified - decide the winner unpredictably. Either a freshly synced database
  gets clobbered back to the shipped baseline, or the shipped baseline is skipped
  and a stale database survives an upgrade. Both are silent.

  Second, integrity. ProgramData is writable by ordinary users until the service
  tightens the ACL on first run. Program Files is not. A detection database that
  an unprivileged process can rewrite is an obvious way to disable detection or,
  worse, to whitelist something - the same threat model that already makes
  WhitelistStore refuse to honour entries unless the data directory is hardened.

  So the MSI ships an immutable baseline it fully owns, and the service seeds or
  refreshes the working copy from it (see SeedDetectionContent). Windows Installer
  never touches runtime state, and the baseline cannot be tampered with by a
  non-administrator. This is also how commercial engines ship: a baseline in the
  install tree, updated content in a writable data directory.

  ATTRIBUTION IS A LICENCE CONDITION, NOT DOCUMENTATION
  ====================================================
  THIRD-PARTY-RULES.md is generated by phantom-sigbuild from the rule pack that
  went into the database, and it is shipped because attribution is a *condition*
  of the licences the included rules are distributed under - DRL 1.1 and
  CC BY-SA 4.0 both require it. Shipping signatures.sdb without it would place the
  product in breach of the terms that make those rules redistributable in the
  first place. It travels with the database for that reason, not as a courtesy.

  GUIDs are permanent across product releases.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:util="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/util">

  <Fragment>

    <!--
      Content directory: [INSTALLFOLDER]\content\
      INSTALLFOLDER is declared in Product.wxs; this Fragment extends it, the
      same way DriverComponent.wxs contributes Drivers\ and Certs\.
    -->
    <DirectoryRef Id="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Directory Id="ContentDir" Name="content"/>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <!--
      ProgramData\ShadowStrike\Data\ - the working directory the service reads
      and writes. ProgramDataSS is declared in Product.wxs.
    -->
    <DirectoryRef Id="ProgramDataSS">
      <Directory Id="ProgramDataData" Name="Data"/>
    </DirectoryRef>

    <!--
      Shipped detection content. signatures.sdb is the KeyPath: it is the file
      whose presence defines whether this component is installed, and it is the
      one the service looks for.

      Note this component deliberately contains no ServiceInstall, registry or
      shortcut side effects. It is pure payload, so a repair restores the
      baseline without disturbing anything else.
    -->
    <Component Id="CmpDetectionContent"
               Directory="ContentDir"
               Guid="{47C7BEEC-D59F-4068-AEBA-A958D69632C3}"
               Bitness="always64">

      <File Id="SignatureDatabaseBaseline"
            Name="signatures.sdb"
            Source="$(var.StagingDir)\Content\signatures.sdb"
            KeyPath="yes"/>

      <File Id="ThirdPartyRulesManifest"
            Name="THIRD-PARTY-RULES.md"
            Source="$(var.StagingDir)\Content\THIRD-PARTY-RULES.md"/>
    </Component>

    <!--
      Data directory.

      The ACL here is deliberately tighter than the one on Logs and Quarantine:
      SYSTEM and Administrators only, with no grant to Users. Those two
      directories give Users read access because a support bundle needs to be
      collectable and a quarantine listing needs to be viewable. This one holds
      the signature database, the whitelist and the feed credentials, and read
      access to a whitelist tells an attacker exactly what will not be scanned
      while any write access would let them add to it.

      The service applies the same restriction itself at first run
      (DataStorePaths::ApplyRestrictiveAcl, which also disables inheritance).
      Setting it here as well is not redundant: it closes the window between the
      installer creating the directory and the service first starting, during
      which the inherited ProgramData ACL would otherwise let any user create
      files in it - including a whitelist.wdb of their own choosing.
    -->
    <Component Id="CmpProgramDataData"
               Directory="ProgramDataData"
               Guid="{E3DF596D-0A1B-4B3F-A529-A9CE11B020A0}"
               Bitness="always64">
      <CreateFolder>
        <util:PermissionEx User="System"
                           Domain="NT AUTHORITY"
                           GenericAll="yes"/>
        <util:PermissionEx User="Administrators"
                           GenericAll="yes"/>
      </CreateFolder>
      <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                     Key="SOFTWARE\ShadowStrike\PhantomHome\Dirs"
                     Name="Data"
                     Type="string"
                     Value="[ProgramDataData]"
                     KeyPath="yes"/>
    </Component>

  </Fragment>
</Wix>
